            <ArticleTitle>Young Adult Perceptions Surrounding Hookah Use.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>593-600</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.14485/HBPR.4.6.9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="Objective" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">We aimed to improve understanding of young adults' perceptions and interpretations of the contexts surrounding use.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Methods" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">We conducted focus groups with young adult hookah users (ages 18-28) to examine hookah-use experiences and risk perceptions.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Results" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Two dominant themes that emerged from the discussions were the social aspects of use (eg, &quot;ending up&quot; at a hookah café while out with friends), and the mental and physiological reactions to use (eg, relaxation and a &quot;high&quot; feeling). Participants often argued that because they only used hookah every few weeks, hookah use was much less dangerous than cigarette use.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Conclusions" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Findings suggest that hookah research should account for heuristic processing and point to several areas where better health communication and stricter regulatory policies are needed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
